#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb1fd90-967a-508f-964a-ddcc8ad1fec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238329} {:answer/id #uuid "38d3c833-662c-50cc-95fd-d02f93ab6c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238545} {:answer/id #uuid "780f23b5-b5bf-596a-b047-13dbffe326e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239094} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aaa345-119e-5e65-bc94-2528387dcd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239795} {:answer/id #uuid "de389670-0e34-52aa-9dc2-f2ea5b75eb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239795} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8418831-a814-5787-ba6f-30266ff0bc9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e735c8c7-7a76-5786-82b2-e7e846ae7fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104731" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30453404-690a-5b85-850f-9aaa09a4efe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} {:answer/id #uuid "35cdd507-7f75-51ad-acf4-534f3b62ea96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238522} {:answer/id #uuid "58c144e5-2b4f-5201-b0bb-e144657253c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238819} {:answer/id #uuid "da6b93e1-2f2d-5dd2-9235-e2c75c2150c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239960} {:answer/id #uuid "e08284d3-5a39-5136-ad35-3aadf74bdc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6c211e-df9c-57af-8d94-442d4251996d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7794dd99-ebef-5626-aacd-8b9fce4aa6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104733" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab61eea-ba71-5224-bbe8-eca2df0fe157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238278} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0e41b7-21e7-573e-b5d2-f356608a6ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238703} {:answer/id #uuid "ace99781-1604-53b3-b08e-f26c241a358f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239557} {:answer/id #uuid "c2892dd7-dcc3-567c-8bcb-ac89416a6e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239762} {:answer/id #uuid "e7de7023-d488-540d-962c-5b17cc9d176b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238278} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ef2ea0-0a2c-50ba-bcc4-0d5182b81264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "86f7c787-e5df-5794-8bba-c906aa91522f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104739" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f31b92-c386-50b2-b977-d94225bcfbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238253} {:answer/id #uuid "465fbdf4-7176-5e2a-afb9-7055988a9594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238665} {:answer/id #uuid "54e54a33-5402-591a-ac33-570a9653db6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} {:answer/id #uuid "796fc17c-d543-5b01-83ae-f85bfdb65ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239102} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd66caa-a9be-51dd-a8aa-57947da3bed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed60cbe-d21d-5310-989e-903acd1a44f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7507d02e-e1da-50a2-9a9d-6236d6a7afae" :question/math-academy-id "q-104745" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d4147a-c71a-5c94-85e5-f5d9a8038924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238114} {:answer/id #uuid "27bb17e7-e9f0-57ab-a950-9c30d0a83e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238398} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a11a7-e4b9-5d99-a4e6-6b05e88774ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} {:answer/id #uuid "6d475b88-50f1-508c-94ec-f40b5b005181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238994} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f3259a-1dbd-5b91-9075-78bd6e17f1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afbb94e-3c3c-582d-befe-a39fc2c5fb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5c03f65-0c80-5089-93df-00e7a5829280" :question/math-academy-id "q-104747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b3b744-39b6-57dd-83b1-ca255a0e5591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238414} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7d66b3-3253-501c-9d23-d0a00aa17af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239429} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a48cb6-3488-5f91-a510-56e98f26b7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{ln}^{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239614} {:answer/id #uuid "d222d7af-bcc2-54b0-bd8f-b14855923fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239900} {:answer/id #uuid "f773a812-93fa-544d-b4f9-b7fcdc706cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239429} :answer-field/id #uuid "61dfdacb-ab42-5b45-8340-88f93ef0c7b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "13fd31c7-58b2-51a7-95e7-235c3524f10b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104761" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{2x{e}^{-y}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2652d9ba-683c-5862-9cda-3f3daeece5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238388} {:answer/id #uuid "61cedf07-823b-5fcb-9c03-378f9a5536db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(e+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238913} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec9781a-d4b2-5ee0-add1-074981a33f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239157} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa1d769-7e7e-5880-bb59-e10f4d9e33b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9(3e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239449} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4b0e5-c5b9-5c22-abf0-49ca0a07f26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239914} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f3d66e-e3ad-5896-806b-677f9fc2a9bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "436ac2b7-bdc3-58c8-9890-0935ce7a366c" :question/math-academy-id "q-104764" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{(-x)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c543d32-f590-5572-b8c8-deb27960d555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=64\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238714} {:answer/id #uuid "700992b4-b842-51c7-b525-693f90bb0074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239023} {:answer/id #uuid "87ba9162-4009-571e-983c-2feb233ecf2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239235} {:answer/id #uuid "970f738f-931d-5d26-9c18-d7b25f79a2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=64\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239360} {:answer/id #uuid "a4846258-86d8-51c8-bd37-7d37844a4897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239235} :answer-field/id #uuid "58635343-ad05-5680-8753-254e9127f052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57c8ce88-a674-5a42-a4cd-edf82238437b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106392" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b4814e27d40b8f1a578f6f51497ce83cc03d2733f3b7827ce94403ae6cecfb7.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eab6327-0c95-54d9-859f-4f150094dce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238312} {:answer/id #uuid "2b534545-458b-5a55-829c-b1c45b99b8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238441} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff810b4-3ac7-5a08-9b8a-a55840c6941e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238745} {:answer/id #uuid "e23ff3a9-5264-5c9c-8110-67cc3d40bc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240045} {:answer/id #uuid "f42b34dd-2ab0-5551-b5c4-510abf73621f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238441} :answer-field/id #uuid "324e0c78-68ac-5d43-adfc-f4c76aad63b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "347a59c3-4995-580b-b51b-93308b9dade8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106393" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3f680832619e0864021c348bd4d5e1f2985d8fa565869f127644232b5ab7657.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d5cace-aeab-5067-989b-312edd83e010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238204} {:answer/id #uuid "677c9520-5ed4-59f1-931c-d2e62fc28d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238956} {:answer/id #uuid "c3755588-e0ab-5823-a746-1d1057922da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239779} {:answer/id #uuid "d27aec81-8aa1-5fb3-80bb-671941d6b040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239902} {:answer/id #uuid "e62c3b7f-4fbd-5447-8273-a59f2b47cfed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240077} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef4e086-16ad-5711-9fa6-49b2da633903" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b013c0a-222b-5140-b73f-3ac6156dca44" :question/math-academy-id "q-106394" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f620063351ced6387988e809439c2bc5853714f94091b1806aad819c5de1a19c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $6,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8bb686-ea32-5b52-9936-7c1664446734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238173} {:answer/id #uuid "4124ebcd-c7c2-5d84-900d-fe07b2efcb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238621} {:answer/id #uuid "8194ab38-7328-5887-b3b9-e1e2f64fbc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239180} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5b9dc0-ea18-5e2a-94ac-614bc1ed4cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240277} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9a3eb1-39b9-5a12-b068-b21c5947d128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238173} :answer-field/id #uuid "6702d3ed-66cf-5f6d-bd9f-9720b46c50a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9b7ccfe2-39a5-5e04-a8cf-7ac6639dc543" :question/math-academy-id "q-106398" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b9f67594871385ccf7ac02c3af9e47a585116c162a08cdc1795d452a09912fe.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-3)$ with horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,-3),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43baf2bf-555a-5a7d-80d6-1e80998846ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238642} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4bf5ea-95a4-51a4-8a22-9d7407ee588f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239118} {:answer/id #uuid "a87502b6-a974-55ef-9731-5c805fa819cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239520} {:answer/id #uuid "a900b4a3-b49d-54e8-8cd3-b7ee4fef1cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239531} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a791a8-6243-5cbb-8e82-29b536b3f82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239520} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefc758a-5b74-5f60-8c85-a8cfa0ef9113" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "face32c2-838a-5a8c-a2dc-dfdf5610f42d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106399" :question/problem "![](images/49/494e360a4c22d96feb811de6ee507996a11be65ee346d837debcb927c0d28434.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,2)$ with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2606c6c4-d56c-5d3e-a967-0885403bd089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238384} {:answer/id #uuid "2b47e0bc-140c-54a7-b7dc-1cb1d3931c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238439} {:answer/id #uuid "445d539a-d68f-529c-b844-0b6c16c2944f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238647} {:answer/id #uuid "780e0718-de40-585e-83fa-96a315349c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239093} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bbac62-d15b-51b9-8267-4186aa048365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238384} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba69530b-e8b7-520d-a710-cd5d9f879561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a5b52bfc-d57a-565e-907c-e10caf711dae" :question/math-academy-id "q-106400" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a04ad6bb8ce108e4f63fddcd5c178ec855a646a14761ad989a9e6c9328ddbd0.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6d/6db43ff41344f292c8cb5805febd175088fe068fe89927bade0c6fc2246c8781.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275cbc97-498b-541f-b490-b72a4ab58e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238396} {:answer/id #uuid "74ffffaa-f33b-547e-93c3-da339f101aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239065} {:answer/id #uuid "97d904bb-b2da-58f1-80e3-6c632732919d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239372} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7be160-4c62-5f6d-9a5c-7c8834e48bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239399} {:answer/id #uuid "b456abd5-51b5-5847-b511-91f87dd5e414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239631} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2a1dfb-0aab-5eed-a08a-01fe0d294c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d3351acb-0711-5b2c-8212-2f838f3d3d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106402" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea3bc202dd0a40cfe096fb4170331fed39de28870aa355c776fd82de3b5db26b.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/74/74714486ad225aba7111329be0c3853b7b922a852df450b057632e73fce5be6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154230c4-e5cc-5eb7-a10c-06c3c17162be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238230} {:answer/id #uuid "283b8e16-5fca-5dfa-9a88-d69fd0c9c6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238407} {:answer/id #uuid "47ee14be-4f73-52f9-be6e-e1119afc63d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238680} {:answer/id #uuid "c72d8441-2566-5fb6-a259-a23948627804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239810} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa97086-0b06-56cd-a813-1ae30f693d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238407} :answer-field/id #uuid "2104c834-96d2-54e9-ad19-6dc6e3eb1a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9cfc762-69e6-5d11-8bd6-6402006ff4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106403" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b344621d5500d3253295c970b0a7557b80f4d2cbfa66c17ba6f8868c1424c08.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/9e/9e236591e21403bb739dafa68a665c8a10eed0e9e8ac104e7b39431f02329c17.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b913d54-324a-5ad8-aa62-3a070b030f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238850} {:answer/id #uuid "c3097672-8b26-512d-acb0-da5b48991b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239772} {:answer/id #uuid "ca724085-3fcc-56b7-9d3e-9b5df0e5c9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239833} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aa8820-6119-5eac-8a02-513174a82ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240049} {:answer/id #uuid "e95fd9a6-9c15-593c-a235-3b994fbad558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240109} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1883e4-6fef-59d0-bdd7-282f4291d66b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fb99db2-aa7f-56fb-932b-79345c0c1bd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106404" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbaad877ff577039a288011f904d856bec1417d995113c4c137f7ac536d44968.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5a/5a028284d29850a9561a589c0e9fbf85e2e10e4e7a23d4ab46a765f492955b4f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2739d8-8263-5715-80a0-b3b7c6968f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238306} {:answer/id #uuid "382f295d-a445-5d8b-bc0f-6ace3caf95de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238540} {:answer/id #uuid "73aae932-0595-5347-8815-ae9f181c5b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239045} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2d8986-d88d-5b5c-9f11-422be6546780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239162} {:answer/id #uuid "c61dff78-fa4e-581e-a2bf-f0e93446cb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239045} :answer-field/id #uuid "898c248a-58ee-5aa9-8564-479985c71b0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f8cd9927-85e7-5cc4-8971-2d06dd8aa5a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106405" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430731d8-2746-561b-9932-67b216b9fe6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238637} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5367ef-504b-5ba3-a55b-c4c01e11de99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238996} {:answer/id #uuid "e04c2016-5bb4-58d5-b9d6-a2cd96a09d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240017} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b0efb3-ded6-535b-9129-9f1e0979660f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240025} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f34c9d-d585-551d-ad3a-8864f684d38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238637} :answer-field/id #uuid "e50ff31d-bfd9-5fd4-b0bd-dcf00ea22347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6677d4ff-0012-5e92-83bc-c53813937d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106406" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c85e08-cf6a-5307-80f4-ce01ca0ebcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238422} {:answer/id #uuid "45c84b8d-f295-5003-a523-bb16c6bcf8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238658} {:answer/id #uuid "689800b2-34c6-5ad7-a7b0-9045f353fb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238964} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4de9d6-48b4-5098-a575-119212df9e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239570} {:answer/id #uuid "c35b39d0-2b50-56f0-b092-5a399f164c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778} :answer-field/id #uuid "45dde714-85df-57b8-888c-f62cc7cc39a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b47425b-d1be-54fd-a4d5-ddff6578df69" :question/math-academy-id "q-106407" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0363f299-ebce-5cd3-a3d9-6edbee6ef1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{(x-2)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238110} {:answer/id #uuid "09798478-efa7-5adb-b8d0-a50d61788683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{(x-2)}^{2}-4{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238149} {:answer/id #uuid "117cba79-f965-5374-92ac-bc6e684c94b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{(x-3)}^{2}+4{(y-2)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238201} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1fc9c6-aa5a-5af7-8fe4-228cbf131588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239567} {:answer/id #uuid "d42beaa6-50c5-5dd1-9084-6b775db8f4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239919} :answer-field/id #uuid "57eda148-ff6d-5235-8674-30b291d678a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f6811d7d-6469-50eb-bfd3-5c7960859395" :question/math-academy-id "q-106408" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042c1518-d1d0-53f1-a0a9-9ae5e2b3810a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241216} {:answer/id #uuid "25fd6840-64da-5046-9a41-17e620e69326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=49\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241509} {:answer/id #uuid "563e46f3-48da-5668-9f2c-ee04318f3af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241959} {:answer/id #uuid "5a30d7e0-b5ad-5034-9b80-e35643ab2fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=49\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241993} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1ac5a1-9bc7-5d0c-8781-d5c76e137fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241216} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9dd5e4-3664-5643-abff-effa155b79f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f59c2c46-d3e0-513d-b9a2-86449659a748" :question/math-academy-id "q-106701" :question/problem "![](images/db/db61966ec9692ab0e21077c616eeb530589e2d8a617891f6c350bc1b5df4351c.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236c316a-eb06-5065-a4d1-9dd521b3608d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241487} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0f6bcf-0c5a-5e52-8622-c4bfbe673b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=64\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242440} {:answer/id #uuid "a456f1d7-6710-55cd-a8e2-148d9413c879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242622} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d31a3f-b072-5e5d-b9af-b1e6a138b20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242770} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc57155-0a54-544b-9c95-1446279f731f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242991} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccfa23b1-f075-58df-bedf-f0a4ec291e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59b706b1-e2ca-5d92-9b24-fa27cdd71036" :question/math-academy-id "q-106704" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5bd3bf7cecf806ab0fc89db59afc5d385064080f47076d13d2ef25650ce39a23.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-2),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e26f54-0545-533f-b436-b187695ffb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241527} {:answer/id #uuid "41e65854-bc6f-5943-80a0-131a575e4339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241770} {:answer/id #uuid "b99b23a2-5fb6-528b-a3a0-8cd47d7beec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242809} {:answer/id #uuid "dffbd024-6452-5872-b010-95e850431a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243171} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6861c8-02be-5144-bb3f-cb952060d258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243387} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ba7a39-59bd-58bf-8f95-ac81f5fdfa4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c82fb7c-60f3-5ec6-bb6a-1161e86d2f27" :question/math-academy-id "q-106711" :question/problem "![](images/84/8498bb189b51954a79f8ca6638631d6acd0a3d4fe86f6f4cecb2de77ee370f94.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/fb/fb686f7295c2c6ac3058361ecc0438ae56d650950833185b17ec959d654cc511.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365c4773-e368-5e9e-b8d3-09d97f8cf201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241658} {:answer/id #uuid "6115395b-4dd0-5476-84c1-62a064355b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242054} {:answer/id #uuid "718e50be-5a17-5e14-88c8-db7be4da154a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242189} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ffe555-2803-571a-a13e-b08b1c573270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242663} {:answer/id #uuid "db656494-d26d-54fd-a2be-e62b928fc054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242054} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7931ea-4640-57b6-8a13-7f713b36ad36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00eda352-d2e8-5490-8b6e-f1ece02f57de" :question/math-academy-id "q-106715" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26da406a-b1ee-533c-8e84-f3f3308fd3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{2}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247767} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c223c6-0129-5a77-a5c1-dbc22604a28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249062} {:answer/id #uuid "b96beb80-9254-5797-a25b-3e85eaa45af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{3{e}^{-3x}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249082} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8237c1-5f1b-5b27-84a1-36bf62527629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{2{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249105} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f75f10-d4ff-5f2b-8f08-23ed364814bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249062} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ddb17e-a21a-5bc8-bdfe-e5654290dd4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a75c4b14-7006-556e-bd1e-e4613f35b446" :question/math-academy-id "q-114287" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x{e}^{-3x}+2\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f62ef64-e190-59a0-80de-2a8c873c7f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247983} {:answer/id #uuid "81cbaf36-0102-5b7f-97ff-d66d8054c9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{4}+\\frac{5{e}^{8x}}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248574} {:answer/id #uuid "99e7a715-8634-5d28-bb4e-237d83c69b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{8}-\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248798} {:answer/id #uuid "afef77b3-81af-530d-a6f6-eb398f03a3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{16}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249004} {:answer/id #uuid "e249a080-5cfd-59dd-bdec-1e4e038339ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x{e}^{8x}}{8}+\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249004} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a43b96e-f2d9-52db-aaf6-60b9ffaaf8e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03919aa8-efb9-58ab-8664-d380cf005c1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114288" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{8x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}-\\int \\frac{1}{8}{e}^{8x}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x{e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{8x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{8x}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{8x}}{16}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022d7b7b-070a-51f5-bd49-94b4db34c49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247437} {:answer/id #uuid "2e34cf00-1e95-5551-a888-a986e9043bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247834} {:answer/id #uuid "76108c8b-8f42-5f4a-9dd2-e847f09e6e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248463} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfdc77f-bbbe-57f4-9329-f07738aa11a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248678} {:answer/id #uuid "c6440ae3-1132-5195-b3a2-356c7752e48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247834} :answer-field/id #uuid "eede1f3b-85f2-5008-b473-3fcd59ae080b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "872e80da-db87-5cbe-89ef-83c58a2b3950" :question/math-academy-id "q-114289" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{3}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′} & ={3}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\int \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04df1fbb-68fc-5df3-a2f3-5de5f64fbd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-3){e}^{x+3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247459} {:answer/id #uuid "059681dd-6855-5de5-adb1-fa9432d5314c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{x}{3}-6){e}^{x+3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247472} {:answer/id #uuid "19b553b9-fab9-540b-be56-77f1ce0d0a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x-1){e}^{x+3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247659} {:answer/id #uuid "c43e1a7b-f3a2-5b76-a623-7db7da101361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+3){e}^{x+3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249166} {:answer/id #uuid "ec744b0d-6761-589d-ad26-06e817b26961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{x}{2}-3){e}^{x+3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247459} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3af4228-49fc-5b51-b5a2-20758a8a7ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "623d728c-8114-50ed-8705-4853f0ddb4f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-114290" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x-2\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{x+3}\\, & ⟹\\,v={e}^{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2)\\cdot {e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-2){e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-\\int {e}^{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-2){e}^{x+3}-{e}^{x+3}+C \\\\  & =(x-3){e}^{x+3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36a625e0-f342-5d88-863b-2cc610f12c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x-1){e}^{-3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247918} {:answer/id #uuid "3e06e8db-4185-544e-9ae5-0b2a2e9f7015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-x){e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247972} {:answer/id #uuid "4d558edc-f058-5d64-aa0f-acff177c0e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2-3x){e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248117} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa27907-71bc-5690-9d5a-5ea98a3a03cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x-1){e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248503} {:answer/id #uuid "cd15176c-1580-5959-a4f3-0332eefaf817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2-x){e}^{-3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247972} :answer-field/id #uuid "f37b9f98-70f3-5724-8992-60b01c3a21f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1022e01f-7a5a-562e-a877-5ba5b3c9edd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114293" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x-4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (3x-4)\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(3x-4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x-4){e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-4){e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x-3){e}^{-3x}}{3}+C \\\\  & =(1-x){e}^{-3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6729e761-a109-51c4-8606-18751a53f95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248350} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf1abf3-9f83-5499-84e8-2cdbf9e21301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248682} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaf0778-2cce-5ff4-8bd6-a8910cbdb116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248985} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3b4c5a-2810-58be-abbd-d2691adfd127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249108} {:answer/id #uuid "c70e5714-4c05-5edb-b661-6e6122f8d589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249108} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7740ab9-c0ff-5f8c-91e3-87881adad76e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8a77892-fbcb-5fb9-802a-ce425028c881" :question/math-academy-id "q-114297" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
